<Page title="How we verify farms and write guides" crumb="How we verify" description="How grassfed.food finds farms, checks each listing against the farm's own website, confirms certifications, and keeps listings current.">
  <p class="lede">A directory is only useful if it's right. Here is exactly what our "Verified" stamp means, and what it doesn't.</p>

  <h2>Where farms come from</h2>
  <p>We find farms through certifier listings (American Grassfed Association, A Greener World), USDA local food data, state agriculture programs, web searches, and farms that ask to be listed. We record facts and link to sources. We don't copy descriptions or photos from other directories.</p>

  <h2>What we check before listing a farm</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>The farm's own website is live and names the farm.</li>
    <li>The website says what the farm sells and how people can buy it: on-farm store, pickup, farmers markets, CSA, shipping, or bulk shares.</li>
    <li>We record how the farm describes its feeding, in the farm's own words, and link to the page where it says so. Farms update their sites, so follow the link for the current wording.</li>
    <li>For each certification the farm mentions, we look the farm up in the certifier's public listing. We show a certification only when we find it there.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>How we confirm USDA Organic</h2>
  <p>Each month USDA publishes a full list of certified organic operations in its <a href="https://organic.ams.usda.gov/integrity/" rel="nofollow">Organic Integrity Database</a>, including exactly what each one is certified for. We match our farms against it by name, website and town, and use three different labels because they mean different things to you:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>USDA Organic:</strong> the farm's animals are certified organic, so its meat, eggs or milk can be sold as organic.</li>
    <li><strong>Organic dairy products:</strong> the farm's milk, butter or cheese is certified organic, even though its animals aren't certified as livestock.</li>
    <li><strong>Organic pasture:</strong> the land the animals graze, or the hay they eat, is certified organic, but the animals are not. Their meat and milk can't be sold as organic, because organic livestock rules also cover feed during finishing, health care and records from birth.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The "USDA Organic" filter shows only the first two. When a match isn't exact, we check it against the farm's own website before any label appears. A farm calling itself organic on its own site is never enough.</p>

  <h2>What "Verified" means</h2>
  <p>The date on each stamp is the last time we completed those checks. It confirms the farm exists and describes itself the way we show it. It does not mean we visited the farm or audited its practices. Only third-party certifiers do that, which is why we show certifications separately.</p>
  <p>We recheck listings regularly. A listing not rechecked within {FRESH_DAYS} days shows "Due for recheck." If we can't confirm a farm for 12 months, we take the listing down.</p>

  <h2>How we label feeding</h2>
  <p>Grass-fed and grass-finished are different claims. Grass-fed says the animals eat grass; grass-finished says they keep eating only grass and forage through their final months before harvest, when many grass-fed cattle are switched to grain. Every farm shows both parts:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Grass-fed, grass-finished:</strong> the farm says its animals eat grass and forage their whole lives.</li>
    <li><strong>Grass-fed, grass-finished available:</strong> the farm sells grass-finished beef and also grain-finished beef. Ask for grass-finished when you order.</li>
    <li><strong>Grass-fed, finishing not stated:</strong> the farm says grass-fed but doesn't say how animals are finished. Ask the farm.</li>
    <li><strong>Grass-fed, grain-finished:</strong> the animals graze but get grain before harvest.</li>
    <li><strong>Partial grass diet:</strong> the farm describes a mixed or percentage diet.</li>
    <li><strong>Pasture-raised, not labeled grass-fed:</strong> the animals live on pasture but the farm doesn't claim a grass-only diet. Typical for pork and poultry.</li>
    <li><strong>Grass-fed herd</strong> or <strong>pasture-based herd:</strong> for dairy farms, where finishing doesn't apply.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Labels follow the farm's own wording. When it is ambiguous, we use the more cautious label.</p>

  <h2>State pages</h2>
  <p>A state guide is shown to search engines once it has at least {INDEX_THRESHOLD} verified farms. Until then you can still browse it, and we keep adding farms.</p>

  <h2>How we write guides and comparisons</h2>
  <p>Our guides are researched and drafted with the help of AI tools, then checked against the sources linked at the end of every article. Every factual claim needs a source. We don't make health claims about grass-fed food, and when we mention nutrition research we say how large the differences actually are.</p>
  <p>Comparisons of delivery companies use each company's published prices, sourcing statements and certifications. We haven't taste-tested the products, and every comparison says so.</p>

  <h2>Independence</h2>
  <p>Farms can't pay to be listed or to rank higher, and no company pays us to be included in a guide. <a href="/disclosure/">How we make money</a></p>
</Page>
